{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CffNeFCm7R237jzvBFbcK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe structure is largely idiomatic and additive — the new `DuplicateTelephoneException` sits in `rest.advice` alongside `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, the exception handler faithfully mirrors the existing handlers, and extracting a `requireUniqueTelephone` helper keeps `addOwner` readable. One concrete issue:\n\n**Duplicated normalization logic in `requireUniqueTelephone` (OwnerRestControllerV1).** The same class already owns `normalizeTelephone(...)`, and `addOwner` calls it one line before invoking this helper. But `requireUniqueTelephone` re-implements the digit-stripping inline with `existing.replaceAll(\"\\\\D\", \"\")` rather than reusing `normalizeTelephone`. This forks the definition of \"normalized telephone\" into two places within a single class — if the normalization rule ever changes (e.g. stripping a leading country code), one site will drift from the other, and the uniqueness check would silently diverge from what `addOwner` actually stores. Normalize the existing owner's value through the same `normalizeTelephone(existing)` call so there is one authoritative definition:\n\n```java\nprivate void requireUniqueTelephone(String telephone) {\n    boolean inUse = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()\n        .map(Owner::getTelephone)\n        .filter(existing -> existing != null)\n        .map(this::normalizeTelephone)\n        .anyMatch(telephone::equals);\n    if (inUse) {\n        throw new DuplicateTelephoneException(telephone);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n(This keeps the fix local to the helper; the `null` guard is still useful since stored legacy values may be null.)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8330,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemera